@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+---
+id: dl-006-applypatch-rejects-a-patch-that-is-not-plain-json-data
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyPatch rejects a patch that is not plain JSON data"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6902 describes a patch as a JSON document: an array of operation objects, each `value` being a JSON
+value. `applyPatch` (dl-004) rejects patches that are not well formed, but its check does not cover the
+JSON data itself. A patch that carries a `Date`, a `NaN`, an `undefined` member, or an operation whose
+`op` or `path` is inherited rather than an own member is currently accepted: the value is copied with
+`structuredClone`, which keeps a `Date` as a `Date` and an `undefined` member as `undefined`, so the
+result is not JSON.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyPatch` throws an `Error` for a patch that is not plain JSON data, with the operation index
+  (for example `patch[2]`) in the message. A patch is plain JSON data when:
+  - each operation is a plain object whose members are own properties;
+  - each `value` is a JSON value: `null`, a string, a boolean, a finite number, an array whose elements
+    are JSON values, or a plain object with own, enumerable string-keyed members that are JSON values.
+- The check runs when the patch is parsed, before any operation is applied, so such a patch is an
+  invalid patch (dl-004) and the document is not touched.
+- The document itself is not checked by this decision.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A patch that a looser library would accept, such as one carrying a `Date`, now fails before it
+  runs. Callers that build patches from richer objects must serialise them first.
+- Checking every `value` costs a walk over the patch, which is small next to the copy of the document.
+- Whether `applyPatch` should also check the document is left open; it is not needed for the patch rules
+  of RFC 6902.
